{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "strict": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "composite": true,
    "declaration": true,
    "emitDeclarationOnly": true,
    "outDir": "dist-types",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    // `node` BELONGS HERE because this package COMPILES Node tests along with the browser code: `include`
    // covers `src/**/*.ts`, and below it sit `lauf-lebenszyklus-grenze.test.ts` and
    // `leistung-contract-form.test.ts`, which use `node:fs`/`node:path`/`__dirname` (they read real files of
    // the tree). Without this entry `tsc` reported five errors — not about the code, but about its own
    // configuration.
    //
    // ⚠️ IT IS AMBIENT FOR THE WHOLE PACKAGE, and a per-file `/// <reference types="node" />` would NOT change
    // that: a triple-slash reference injects its global declarations into the SAME program, it does not scope
    // them to the file that carries it.
    //
    // MEASURED 2026-09-11 rather than argued. `node` was removed from `types` and the reference written into
    // the two Node tests instead: `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` stayed green (0 errors) — AND a probe line
    // reading `process.platform` in `src/format.ts`, a browser file carrying NO reference, compiled clean too.
    // The globals reach every file of the program either way. The narrower-looking alternative therefore buys
    // locality of the declaration, not isolation, and pays for it with a second place to keep in step. The
    // entry stays here, where the reason can be read.
    "types": ["react", "react-dom", "node"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.tsx"],
  "exclude": ["src/stories"]
}
